            <title><![CDATA[Java4Shell: RCE in OpenJDK when processing Base64 (177 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://calif.io/java4shell/</link>
            <guid>50538240</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:11:28 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://calif.io/java4shell/">https://calif.io/java4shell/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50538240">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
          <article id="technical-writeup">
            <div>
              <h2>The Tail of Base64</h2>
              <p>By Quang Luong</p>
              <dl></dl>
<p>On affected OpenJDK builds, malformed Base64 data can cause a constant-value overwrite of allocated heap memory. Through remote Heap Feng Shui, we turn that overwrite into a heap leak and remote code execution.</p>
<h3 id="discovery">Discovery</h3>
<p>A few months back, I was developing a 0-click exploit on a certain phone vendor. The bug was constrained: it is a linear out-of-bounds write and we have no way to bypass ASLR. I almost considered it unexploitable until I audited the Android Runtime. Turns out, it has some predictable memory allocation pattern and if you align the registers from the starting memory corruption just right you can make the runtime interpret attacker's bytecode.</p>

<p>After completing that exploit chain, inspired (and bored), I decided to look into some thing completely different. So I pulled the official JVM implementation (OpenJDK HotSpot) and started auditing. Java is a mature platform, so I was aware that having a "good" bug here would be hard, but I wanted to give it my best attempt anyway.</p>
<p>Recalling my earlier lesson that complex systems and code crossing multiple boundaries are difficult to get right, I focused the audit on the JIT compiler, intrinsics, widely accessible APIs, and cryptography. Well, more precisely, I told Codex to look at those areas like any serious security researcher in 2026. To be honest, I didn't expect to find anything trivially exploitable, perhaps only some logic flaws or side-channel issues.</p>
<p>Most early results Codex brought me were slops, either unreachable or the requirement of exploitation is not realistic at all. This looks like a gamble I am going to lose. But you know what, fortune favors the bold. So I just prompted harder...</p>
<p>Eventually, Codex brought me a finding for a memory corruption in the Base64 decoder itself. I came looking for copper and found gold, through sheer persistence. But wait, is it really gold, or just fool's gold?</p>

<h3 id="the-vulnerability">The vulnerability</h3>
<p>The issue lies in OpenJDK HotSpot's x86-64 Base64 intrinsic. C2 is the most aggressive JIT compiler in the Java Virtual Machine. Specifically, if you trigger a function repeatedly, then it will compile that function into native code. Even then, ordinary native code itself is not fast enough. Modern CPUs have specialized instructions that allow processing data even faster. HotSpot uses them in optimized routines called intrinsics. The x86-64 Base64 intrinsic miscalculated the padded tail's store mask, causing an out-of-bounds write.</p>
<p>The x86-64 Base64 intrinsic iterates through the input in 256-byte blocks; if it is less than 256, then it will do so in 64-byte blocks. When the input is less than 64 bytes, it uses <code>evmovdqub</code>, the intrinsic for the <code>VMOVDQU8</code> instruction. This instruction takes a 64-bit mask that decides which bytes are read from or written to memory. The mask is first calculated from the input and output length, then updated to reflect the number of padding bytes (<code>'='</code>) in the Base64-encoded string. This is shown in the following (pseudocode):</p>
<div><pre><code>// Zero High Bits Starting with Specified Bit Position
input_mask := bzhiq(-1, input_length)
output_len := (input_length / 4) * 3

// handle padding
if input[input_length - 1] == '=':
    output_len -= 1
    input_mask &gt;&gt;= 1
    if input[input_length - 2] == '=':
        output_len -= 1
        input_mask &gt;&gt;= 1

output_mask := bzhiq(-1, output_len)</code></pre></div>
<p>In the above code, if the remaining <code>input</code> is of the form <code>=, ==, X=, X==, XY=</code>, or <code>===</code>, then <code>output_len</code> will be computed as zero. However, since there is padding, <code>output_len</code> is decremented, resulting in an underflowed value of <code>-1</code> or <code>-2</code>. Since <code>bzhiq(-1, output_len)</code> doesn’t change any bits if <code>output_len &gt;= 64</code>, this results in <code>output_mask == -1u64</code>.</p>
<p>Later the code uses <code>evmovdqub</code> to read and write multiple bytes at a time. For a write, if the mask bit is <code>1</code>, the byte at that index in the vector register is written to memory. So when <code>output_mask == -1u64</code>, all <code>64</code> bytes are written during the following operation:</p>
<div><pre><code>evmovdqub(
	Address(dest, dp) /* Address: destination address addr */,
	k1 /* k1 == output_mask */,
	tmp /* XMMRegister src register/value to write */,
	true,
	Assembler::AVX_512bit /* vector length */
);</code></pre></div>
<p>The generated stub returns the number of bytes it successfully decoded, and returns early when it detects invalid input. The remaining bytes are handled by the Java code, which throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> if the input is invalid.</p>
<p>At runtime, the decoded buffer length is calculated as <code>(ceil(inp_len / 4) * 3) - padding</code>. When the bug is triggered, with <code>65</code> bytes (<code>[64 bytes] + '='</code>) as input, that calculation allocates a <code>50</code>-byte buffer. During decoding the bug makes <code>output_mask == -1</code> for <code>evmovdqub</code>, so <code>64</code> bytes are written starting at offset <code>48</code>, an out-of-bounds write past the end of the buffer.</p>
<p>On OpenJDK 17, the trigger input is <code>'A' * 128 + '='</code> instead of <code>'A' * 64 + '='</code>, because the length threshold to reach the code path is higher.</p>
<h3 id="exploitation-strategy">Exploitation strategy</h3>
<p>The Base64 bug was gold, but not the type that sits on the riverbed waiting to be panned out. It was the type that was deep, dark, and hard to access. While we had memory corruption, exploiting it looked difficult, perhaps even borderline unexploitable, since it only wrote fixed bytes to the heap. Only the landing position of the out-of-bounds write is controllable.</p>
<p>We get the following 64 bytes write starting from the position in buffer where the remaining sets of Base64 decoded bytes would have been written to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Full controlled write to byte 0 (in bound)</li>
<li>4-bit controlled write to byte 1 (in bound)</li>
<li>Uncontrolled write to bytes [2, 63]. For bytes <code>48..63</code>, the value will always be <code>0x9a</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The uncontrolled out-of-bounds write turned out not to be a big problem. We reused a common JavaScript engine exploitation primitive, the classic technique of overwriting a buffer length.</p>
<p>We call the buffer whose length we overwrite the <code>writer</code>. Once its length is corrupted, it keeps accepting our chosen bytes, so if we place another array or object after it, we can corrupt that neighbor with all of our chosen values. That upgrades the fixed write into a bounded, chosen-value linear write.</p>
<p>If we then corrupt another array's length and its data is copied back into the response, we have built a heap disclosure primitive, the <code>reader</code>, from the out-of-bounds read. At this point, before any code execution, we already had JavaBleed, the ability to leak contents from the Java heap, which may hold PII, private keys, other secret keys, and tokens.</p>
<p>From the <code>writer</code> and <code>reader</code>, the rest of this write-up builds the full attack. We repair the objects the linear write damaged to keep the JVM running, mount data-only attacks on application logic, and escalate the bounded write into arbitrary writes that reach the Code Cache and JVM hooks for code execution.</p>
<p>But first things first, we need to align the heap to our advantage.</p>

<h3 id="heap-feng-shui-grooming">Heap Feng Shui (Grooming)</h3>
<p><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/17/gctuning/garbage-first-g1-garbage-collector1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">G1 divides the heap into fixed-size regions</a>. Before the region arithmetic matters, the critical arrays have to leave their threads' private allocation ranges.</p>
<p>HotSpot normally gives each Java thread a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/java.html#advanced-garbage-collection-options-for-java" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">thread-local allocation buffer (TLAB)</a>, a private slice of Eden. Objects created by different request threads advance different cursors, which gives us no useful order between a Base64 decoder in one TLAB and a writer in another.</p>
<p>The first grooming layer controls the allocation route. The critical array's size and the space left in the worker's TLAB decide whether HotSpot refills that TLAB or takes the attacker-preferred <a href="https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/jdk-21%2B35/src/hotspot/share/gc/shared/memAllocator.cpp#L268-L360" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">outside-TLAB allocation path</a>.</p>
<p>The second layer controls the layout. However, we do not choose how G1 allocates memory, and we don't know the current heap state. To approach this limitation, Calif used the tried and tested method of Spray and Pray.</p>
<p>Object sizes and padding fix offsets inside region-sized spans. We spray several live writer and reader pairs to give the decoder output several possible neighbors. When its region lands immediately below a writer region, the widened reader produces a larger application response. We discard misses and retry until successful.</p>
<p>One early challenge during exploit development was that the AI would get stuck during heap grooming primitive development. In order to save tokens and time, Calif wrote a small heap tracer and visualizer to give the agent information about the heap state, combined with our expertise on specific cases.</p>
<h3 id="remote-heap-feng-shui">Remote Heap Feng Shui</h3>
<p>In order to perform remote grooming, each request data would be sent up until the critical heap allocation would occur. By ensuring the data that was previously sent had a TCP acknowledgement, the exploit would have control over the allocations up to date. Once we were confident our data so far is buffered on the target, we then sent the rest, completing the http request. This allows both heap allocation control and more precise schedule as delivering small network packets is more stable than big ones.</p>

<p>The exact allocation and free order schedule belongs to the targeted application profile, while we are able to control the adding, writer, reader, and decoder pieces.</p>
<p>The current attack is sensitive to network conditions, like RTT and jitter. Low, stable round-trip times are preferred for a more reliable attack. In our controlled environment, we performed this attack over the internet through public IP addresses instead of just assuming perfect laboratory conditions. For the current attack path to achieve optimal stability and reliability in exploitation, Calif turned to physics and utilized money to solve the problem, rather than experimenting with TCP/IP stack configuration. In other words, Calif rented another VM in the same region as our target.</p>
<h3 id="warm-up-rehearsal-and-retries">Warm-up, rehearsal, and retries</h3>
<p>To summarize the chain so far, we will warm up the Base64 decoder to C2 tier. We can rehearse the exploit run to calibrate timing and responses against the target before causing corruption to occur. Once the required information has been obtained, we can trigger the vulnerability. Incorrect placement could cause the JVM to crash, but in many instances, we can simply try again.</p>
<dl><p><dt>WARM</dt><dd>Run valid decodes until the JIT selects the affected fast path. No memory is corrupted.</dd></p><p><dt>REHEARSE</dt><dd>Optional safe round for calibration.</dd></p><p><dt>TRIGGER</dt><dd>Send the malformed tail. If the objects line up, the fixed spill enlarges the writer.</dd></p><p><dt>DISCLOSE</dt><dd>Continue the writer so chosen bytes enlarge the reader, then collect the response-derived heap bytes.</dd></p><p><dt>REPAIR</dt><dd>Use the disclosed originals to restore temporary headers and untouched neighbors when the chain has a repair path.</dd></p><p><dt>FINISH</dt><dd>Make the application-specific change and verify it through a live request.</dd></p></dl>
<h3 id="limitations">Limitations</h3>
<p><strong>Request streaming.</strong> Some current grooming profiles need partial request bodies to reach the application. A proxy that waits for the complete body removes their release timing. <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_request_buffering" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">nginx buffers request bodies by default</a>.</p>
<p>Request buffering is not a general mitigation. Elasticsearch runs from one complete request, so this setting does not stop that chain. After a configured <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/websocket.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WebSocket upgrade</a>, later frames pass through a tunnel and request-body buffering no longer applies to them.</p>
<p><strong>Application profiles.</strong> There is no universal payload. Each chain needs a decoder route, a live writer, a returned reader or another feedback path, a heap layout, and an application action. Every target needs a bespoke profile. The exploit is not a universal point-and-shoot.</p>
<h3 id="poc">PoC</h3>
<p>Here is a self-contained proof of concept. It warms the Base64 decoder until the vulnerable intrinsic is used, then decodes the malformed <code>64 × "A" + "="</code> input into a 4096-byte array. On an affected JDK it prints <code>REPRODUCED</code>: the decoder rejects the input, but 62 bytes (offsets 50 to 111) past the decoded output have already been overwritten. Every write lands inside a real array, so the trigger is safe to run.</p>
<div><pre><code>import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Base64;

/** Bounded standalone trigger: every write stays inside a real 4096-byte array. */
public final class Base64Trigger {
    private static volatile int sink;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Base64.Decoder decoder = Base64.getDecoder();
        byte[] valid = "A".repeat(64).getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);
        byte[] malformed = ("A".repeat(64) + "=").getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);
        byte[] target = new byte[4096];

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 200_000; i++) {
            sink += decoder.decode(valid, target);
        }

        Arrays.fill(target, (byte) 0x5a);
        boolean rejected = false;
        try {
            decoder.decode(malformed, target);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException expected) {
            rejected = true;
        }

        int changed = 0;
        int first = -1;
        int last = -1;
        for (int i = 50; i &lt; target.length; i++) {
            if (target[i] != (byte) 0x5a) {
                changed++;
                if (first &lt; 0) first = i;
                last = i;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("runtime=" + System.getProperty("java.runtime.version"));
        System.out.println("vm=" + System.getProperty("java.vm.name"));
        System.out.printf("rejected=%s changed=%d first=%d last=%d%n",
            rejected, changed, first, last);

        if (!rejected || changed != 62 || first != 50 || last != 111) {
            System.err.println("NOT REPRODUCED");
            System.exit(1);
        }
        System.out.println("REPRODUCED");
    }
}</code></pre></div>
<p>We are holding back the application-specific exploits for now. This write-up covers the vulnerability and the general exploitation technique. The weaponized chains for individual products stay private until everyone has had a chance to upgrade. We will publish them later.</p>
<h3 id="affected-products">Affected products</h3>
<p>Java4Shell affects OpenJDK from version 17.0.6 or newer.</p>
<p>Because they are built from OpenJDK, the following Java distributions are also vulnerable:</p>
<ul>
<li>Amazon Corretto</li>
<li>Eclipse Temurin</li>
<li>Microsoft Build of OpenJDK</li>
<li>Azul Zulu</li>
<li>BellSoft Liberica</li>
<li>SAP SapMachine</li>
<li>Red Hat OpenJDK</li>
<li>Oracle OpenJDK</li>
<li>Oracle Java SE</li>
<li>Oracle GraalVM for JDK</li>
</ul>
<p>According to Oracle, the following Oracle Java SE versions are vulnerable: 8u511b33-perf, 17.0.21.0.3, 21.0.13.0.3, 25.0.5.0.3, 27.0.1.0.3. We thank Oracle for providing this information ahead of publication time.</p>
<p>Because the vulnerable code is hardware-specific, Java4Shell also requires an x86-64 processor with AVX-512 VBMI support. ARM64 CPUs are not affected.</p>
<p>These mainstream CPU families meet the hardware requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li>Intel Xeon: Ice Lake, Sapphire Rapids, Emerald Rapids, Granite Rapids</li>
<li>AMD EPYC and Ryzen: Zen 4 or newer</li>
</ul>
<p>On Linux, you can check whether your CPU is vulnerable with this command:</p>
<div><pre><code>grep -m1 '^flags' /proc/cpuinfo | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -E '^(avx512f|avx512bw|avx512vbmi|bmi2)$'</code></pre></div>
<p>The output must include all four flags.</p>
<h3 id="recommendations">Recommendations</h3>
<p>OpenJDK released <a href="https://openjdk.org/groups/vulnerability/advisories/2026-11-17" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a security advisory</a> and Oracle released <a href="https://www.oracle.com/security-alerts/cspunov2026.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a patch</a> at 1:00pm PT on Nov 17, 2026.</p>
<p>All Java users should update immediately.</p>
<p>You can also mitigate Java4Shell by starting OpenJDK with either:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>-XX:-UseBASE64Intrinsics</code> to disable hardware-accelerated Base64 encoding and decoding, or</li>
<li><code>-XX:UseAVX=2</code> to disable AVX-512. Apply this flag only on x86-64 hosts. OpenJDK on ARM doesn't recognize it and will fail to start.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="credits">Credits</h3>
<p>Research, write-up and exploits by Quang Luong.</p>
<p>Exploit improvement by Jenish Rakholiya.</p>
<p>Artwork by Michal Zalewski.</p>

