|=---------------------=[  September 30, 2026  ]=------------------------=|
|=-----------------------------------------------------------------------=|
</pre><h3 id="---past-"><a href="#---past-">--[ Past ]</a></h3><p>Last year marked the 40th anniversary of Phrack so we went big. We published<br>our anniversary launch release in print at Defcon 33, HOPE, WHY25 and some<br>additional variations at several conferences around the world including<br>AlligatorCon, B-Sides Canberra, Out of the Box, Datenspuren, DistrictCon,<br>OffensiveCon, Recon, and Toorcamp. We included a CTF with exploit challenges<br>and had puzzles and easter eggs included in the zine. We threw parties and<br>had talks at Defcon and WHY25 to celebrate this milestone.</p><p>More importantly, we got new research into people's hands. The point of the<br>relaunch was to get people reading, researching, writing, and contributing<br>again. We wanted the next person picking up Phrack to find something that<br>made them sit down at a computer and start pulling a system apart.</p><p>We owe a huge thanks to the authors, editors, artists, publishing partners,<br>donors, and staff who made it happen. Writing the paper, working through<br>revisions, putting the print editions together, paying the bills, and<br>getting boxes of zines to the right places all take time and effort. Most of<br>that work is invisible when you are holding the finished issue. We<br>appreciate everyone who did it.</p><p>And to the readers: thank you for giving a shit. Seeing someone get excited<br>about an article, connect it to something they are working on, or realize<br>that they could contribute something themselves makes the effort worthwhile.<br>That exchange is how this thing keeps going. The relaunch worked, and this<br>issue is the follow-through.</p><h3 id="---present-"><a href="#---present-">--[ Present ]</a></h3><p>This is the third release curated by the new staff, and we are not slowing<br>down. This issue is larger than last year's, with 23 main papers and eight<br>additional articles in Line Noise. The subjects range from exploitation and<br>reverse engineering to machine learning, hardware, and the history and<br>culture that grew around this work.</p><p>We have container escapes through the vDSO, use-after-free exploitation, QNX<br>userland shellcode, Linux kernel research, and a Chromium renderer sandbox<br>escape through Skia. Elsewhere, the issue takes on Windows smart card<br>architecture, static taint analysis in Binary Ninja, and autonomous<br>underwater vehicles.&nbsp;&nbsp;There is also work examining backdoors in machine<br>learning and what it means to trust an AI system. One paper takes us from<br>PhrackCTF to two WinFSP zero-days, which is a pretty good argument for<br>continuing to put challenges in a zine.</p><p>We also have four more articles in Phrack World History, including the story<br>of the infamous SSH1 CRC32 vulnerability featured in The Matrix Reloaded as<br>well as stories about blue boxing free phone calls, a confession of a hacker<br>who tried to backdoor Linux kernel in 2003, and an early discovery of<br>botnets for hire. Our Prophile features Halvar Flake, whose contributions to<br>reverse engineering and security research are legendary.</p><h3 id="---future-"><a href="#---future-">--[ Future ]</a></h3><p>In 2026, we find ourselves in a time of rapid technological change. It is<br>hard to say what vulnerability research or red teaming will look like in<br>another five years. AI has moved well beyond completing a line of code into<br>agentic systems capable of analyzing source code and reverse engineering<br>binaries to find and exploit vulnerabilities.</p><p>We can be sure that the future will require us to continue to explore new<br>ideas, create new tools, and push the boundaries of what is possible in the<br>face of new challenges and opportunities created by the advancement of AI<br>and automation systems. As the effort required to put ideas into practice<br>falls, creativity and human intuition forged through experience is valued<br>even higher.</p><p>We hope that this release of Phrack will inspire new ideas, discussions, and<br>collaborations that will keep the human side of hacking a critical element<br>in shaping the future of technology and society. We will continue to do our<br>part to support the community and provide a platform for sharing knowledge<br>and ideas. We look forward to seeing what the future holds and we are<br>excited to be a part of it.</p><p>Read the papers, try the ideas, and challenge the conclusions. When you find<br>something worth sharing, write it up and send it in. We have another issue<br>to put together, and some of you should be in it!</p><h3 id="---greetz-"><a href="#---greetz-">--[ Greetz ]</a></h3><p>To those who have supported us the past three years helping to restore<br>vitality to Phrack, we thank you.</p><p>Conferences who funded prints: Defcon, OffensiveCon, Recon, Unprompted[AU],<br>Unprompted[US], BSides Canberra, Alligatorcon, TenguCon</p><p>Sponsors &amp; Major Donors - WatchTowr, Elltam, Verichains, BShield,<br>TrustedSec, Binarly, Trenchant, Praetorian (WWSUL), EFF, Blaze, EMX,<br>Mullvad, ToorCon, Hacker Initiative, Endrazine, Dug Song, Napster, halvar,<br>Horizon, Huku, Julio Auto, Gadi, and the rest of the people who stepped up with<br>smaller donations.</p><p>Artists - Ian Dash, pinguino, tainted, pepper, mar, xer0, abstrakt, ToscaKaiser,<br>h7, zir, Mick, p0rtL, netspooky, ackmage, digitalis, Tedsig42, and the many<br>others who submitted their work for consideration.</p><p>Reviewers - barbie, bsdaemon, C, chompie, danielcuthbert, devinc, grugq, joern,<br>jordan9001, K3, richinseattle, roddux, saber, skyper, SSHad0w, TMZ, zh4ck</p><p>All the authors past and present who are now bound to the history of phrack.</p><p>The unnamed ones who have helped us with logistics and carrying literal tons<br>of paper around conferences to deliver them into the hands of the next<br>generation.</p><h3 id="---disclaimer-"><a href="#---disclaimer-">--[ Disclaimer ]</a></h3><p>All information in Phrack Magazine is, to the best of the ability of<br>the editors and contributors, truthful and accurate. When possible,<br>all facts are checked, all code is compiled. However, we are not<br>omniscient (hell, we don’t even get paid). It is entirely possible<br>something contained within this publication is incorrect in some way.<br>If this is the case, please drop us some email so that we can correct<br>it in a future issue.</p><p>Also, keep in mind that Phrack Magazine accepts no responsibility for<br>the entirely stupid (or illegal) things people may do with the<br>information contained herein. Phrack is a compendium of knowledge,<br>wisdom, wit, and sass. We neither advocate, condone nor participate<br>in any sort of illicit behavior. But we will sit back and watch.</p><p>Lastly, it bears mentioning that the opinions that may be expressed in<br>the articles of Phrack Magazine are intellectual property of their authors.<br>These opinions do not necessarily represent those of the Phrack Staff.</p><h3 id="---contact-"><a href="#---contact-">--[ Contact ]</a></h3><pre>    &lt;  Editors           : staff[at]phrack{dot}org         &gt;
    &gt;  Submissions       : submissions[at]phrack{dot}org   &lt;
    &lt;  /dev/urandom      : loopback[at]phrack{dot}org      &gt;
    &gt;  Arts &amp; Leisure    : arts[at]phrack{dot}org          &lt;
    &lt;  Twitter/X         : phrack                          &gt;
    &gt;  Mastodon          : phrack[at]haunted{dot}computer  &lt;
    &lt;  BlueSky           : phrack{dot}org                  &gt;

ANTISPAM in the subject or face the mighty /dev/null demon!
</pre><br><pre>|=[ EOF ]=---------------------------------------------------------------=|
</pre></div></div>]]></description>
