        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[New Evidence Shows School Phone Bans Are Gradually Improving Test Scores (1063 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/upshot/school-phone-bans-study-scores.html</link>
            <guid>50530163</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 11:45:51 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/upshot/school-phone-bans-study-scores.html">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/upshot/school-phone-bans-study-scores.html</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50530163">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="site-content" data-paywall-inert=""><div id="in-story-masthead"><p><span><a href="https://www.nytimes.com/section/upshot">The Upshot</a></span><span>|</span><span>New Evidence Shows School Phone Bans Are Gradually Improving Test Scores</span></p><div><p><span>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/17/upshot/school-phone-bans-study-scores.html</span></p></div></div><article id="story"><div id="top-wrapper"><p>Advertisement</p><p><a href="#after-top">SKIP ADVERTISEMENT</a></p></div><div aria-live="polite"><p>You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load.</p></div><header><p id="article-summary">Previous results were mixed, but the first national study to track individual students’ growth shows bans may help reverse declines.</p><div data-testid="imageblock-wrapper"><figure aria-label="media" role="group"><div data-testid="imageContainer-children-Image"><picture><source media="(max-width: 599px) and (min-device-pixel-ratio: 3),(max-width: 599px) and (min-resolution: 3dppx),(max-width: 599px) and (min-resolution: 288dpi)" srcset="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-mobileMasterAt3x.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif&amp;disable=upscale&amp;width=1800"><source media="(max-width: 599px) and (min-device-pixel-ratio: 2),(max-width: 599px) and (min-resolution: 2dppx),(max-width: 599px) and (min-resolution: 192dpi)" srcset="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-mobileMasterAt3x.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif&amp;disable=upscale&amp;width=1200"><source media="(max-width: 599px) and (min-device-pixel-ratio: 1),(max-width: 599px) and (min-resolution: 1dppx),(max-width: 599px) and (min-resolution: 96dpi)" srcset="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-mobileMasterAt3x.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif&amp;disable=upscale&amp;width=600"><img alt="Phones with colorful cases, including one covered in glittering gems, rest in plastic pouches of a hanging organizer." src="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-articleLarge.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif&amp;disable=upscale" srcset="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-articleLarge.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif 600w,https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-jumbo.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif 1024w,https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/17/multimedia/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf/17up-phone-pouch1-jlkf-superJumbo.jpg?quality=75&amp;auto=avif 2048w" sizes="((min-width: 600px) and (max-width: 1004px)) 84vw, (min-width: 1005px) 60vw, 100vw" decoding="async" width="600" height="532"></picture></div><figcaption data-testid="photoviewer-children-ImageCaption"><span>The study showed gains in both math and reading, for students at all levels of achievement.</span><span><span>Credit...</span><span><span aria-hidden="false">Photo Illustration by Tonje Thilesen for The New York Times</span></span></span></figcaption></figure></div><p><time datetime="2026-11-17T05:04:03-05:00">Nov. 17, 2026, <span>5:04 a.m. ET</span></time></p></header><section name="articleBody" data-paywall-inert=""><div data-testid="companionColumn-0"><p>School cellphone bans are beginning to modestly improve students’ academic performance, according to the first large national analysis that compared students’ test scores before and after the bans.</p><p>Schools with strict bans — requiring students to lock their phones in <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/25/style/yondr-pouch-school-phone-ban.html" title="">pouches</a> all school day — saw math and reading scores improve as if students had an additional week or two of school a year, <a href="https://cepr.harvard.edu/phone-free-schools" title="" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">the study</a> found. It was published Tuesday by the Center for Education Policy Research at Harvard.</p><p>U.S. students’ test scores <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/13/upshot/test-scores-school-districts-us.html" title="">began falling over a decade ago</a>. The drop coincided with the rise of smartphones, raising questions about their role in the decline. The evidence so far on the academic effects of phone bans <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/04/us/did-school-cellphone-bans-study.html" title="">has been mixed</a>. But the new study indicates they are starting to have an influence.</p><p>“The earlier studies on cellphone bans have been dismissed as implying small impacts, and in-school cellphone use has been ruled out as playing a big role in the decline,” said Thomas Kane, a professor at the Harvard Graduate School of Education and an author of the study. “But we think that’s mistaken.”</p></div><div data-testid="companionColumn-1"><p>The study looked at 3.3 million students between 2021 and 2026. They were in third through 10th grade, attending 4,600 U.S. schools. Using scores on <a href="https://www.nwea.org/" title="" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">MAP tests</a>, which track achievement from fall to spring, it compared the performance of students at schools that put in strict phone bans with those at similar schools that did not have such bans.</p><p>The annual gains reversed about half the annual loss that an average student experienced in the years leading up to the pandemic. (Students lost even more ground during pandemic closures.)</p><div><div id="optimistic-truncator-a11y" tabindex="-1"><hr><p>Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and&nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.nytimes.com/auth/login?response_type=cookie&amp;client_id=vi&amp;redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2026%2F11%2F17%2Fupshot%2Fschool-phone-bans-study-scores.html&amp;asset=opttrunc">log into</a>&nbsp;your Times account, or&nbsp;<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/subscription?campaignId=89WYR&amp;redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2026%2F11%2F17%2Fupshot%2Fschool-phone-bans-study-scores.html">subscribe</a>&nbsp;for all of The Times.</p><hr></div><div data-testid="optimistic-truncator-content"><p data-tpl="t">Thank you for your patience while we verify access.</p><p data-tpl="t">Already a subscriber?&nbsp;<a data-testid="log-in-link" href="https://myaccount.nytimes.com/auth/login?response_type=cookie&amp;client_id=vi&amp;redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2026%2F11%2F17%2Fupshot%2Fschool-phone-bans-study-scores.html&amp;asset=opttrunc">Log in</a>.</p><p data-tpl="t">Want all of The Times?&nbsp;<a data-testid="subscribe-link" href="https://www.nytimes.com/subscription?campaignId=89WYR&amp;redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2026%2F11%2F17%2Fupshot%2Fschool-phone-bans-study-scores.html">Subscribe</a>.</p></div></div></div></section><div data-paywall-inert=""><section aria-labelledby="recirculation-title-placeholder" data-testid="recirculation-placeholder" id="bottom-sheet-sensor"><h2 id="recirculation-title-placeholder">Related Content</h2></section><div id="bottom-wrapper"><p>Advertisement</p><p><a href="#after-bottom">SKIP ADVERTISEMENT</a></p></div></div></article></div></div>]]></description>
