<p>The earlier <code>webshell.lol</code> wave, through Kissmetrics, StorifyMe and Minute Media, affected other sites and isn't included above.</p><h2 id="how-report-uri-would-have-detected-or-even-prevented-this-attack">How Report URI would have detected, or even prevented, this attack</h2><p>Every one of these needs only a CSP in Report-Only mode, so nothing on the site breaks:</p><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Product</th>
<th>What it would have flagged</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href="https://report-uri.com/features/script-watch?ref=blog.report-uri.com">Script Watch</a></strong></td>
<td>A new script dependency on your site: <code>https://1688360.cc/track.js</code>, followed by the <code>.icu</code> iOS routers.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href="https://report-uri.com/features/data-watch?ref=blog.report-uri.com">Data Watch</a></strong></td>
<td>New data destinations: <code>1688360.cc/hit.php</code> and <code>1688360.cc/card.php</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href="https://report-uri.com/features/frame-watch?ref=blog.report-uri.com">Frame Watch</a></strong></td>
<td>A new frame source: <code>eil786npto9u5bq.icu</code>, the exploit-kit iframe.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href="https://report-uri.com/features/csp-integrity?ref=blog.report-uri.com">CSP Integrity</a> + <a href="https://report-uri.com/features/js-time-machine?ref=blog.report-uri.com">JS Time Machine</a> + <a href="https://report-uri.com/features/script-vault?ref=blog.report-uri.com">Script Vault</a></strong></td>
<td>Your Optimizely snippet's fingerprint changing, plus a brand-new script with no matching known file. The JS Time Machine shows when the new version first appeared and how long it was served.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong><a href="https://report-uri.com/features/threat-intelligence?ref=blog.report-uri.com">Threat Intelligence</a></strong></td>
<td>Reports naming <code>5vobauua3ojpklx.icu</code> and <code>webshell.lol</code> are already flagged as IoCs, as are <code>1688360.cc</code> and the other hosts below once they were added.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>It's possible to go even further and deploy a CSP that could actively block these attacks and prevent any data breach happening. The websites affected by this attack weren't necessarily running vulnerable code of their own, they were loading JavaScript from a provider they trusted, and that trust was exploited. This is why monitoring what runs in your visitors' browsers is so important. Report URI gives organisations the visibility to detect when trusted dependencies change or behave unexpectedly, and the tools to help enforce policies that prevent malicious scripts and connections from succeeding.</p><h2 id="did-content-security-policy-help">Did Content Security Policy help?</h2><p>We crawled 256 of the affected organisations' own hosts and evaluated their CSP against what this payload needs:</p><ol><li><strong>Load <code>track.js</code></strong> from <code>1688360.cc</code>.</li><li><strong>Or run the inline fallback</strong>, which needs <code>'unsafe-eval'</code> or <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>.</li><li><strong>Send stolen data</strong> to <code>1688360.cc</code> (<code>connect-src</code>).</li><li><strong>Load the iOS kit</strong> (<code>script-src</code>, <code>frame-src</code>).</li></ol><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Result</th>
<th>Organisations</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>No CSP at all</td>
<td><strong>68</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CSP allowed the payload <strong>and</strong> the data theft</td>
<td><strong>50</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payload ran, but <code>connect-src</code> blocked the data theft (the address swapping still worked)</td>
<td><strong>17</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Payload blocked</strong></td>
<td><strong>24 (15%)</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Allowing <code>cdn.optimizely.com</code> was never going to help.</strong> The malicious code arrived from the trusted host itself. What separated the 24 protected sites from the rest was a restrictive <code>script-src</code>. A site that lists only the hosts it needs blocks <code>1688360.cc</code>. Two common settings let it straight through:</p><ul><li><strong><code>'strict-dynamic'</code></strong>: any script the trusted Optimizely snippet inserts is trusted too.</li><li><code><strong>'unsafe-eval'</strong></code>, which Optimizely's own documentation tells customers to allow: the inline-fallback version ran through <code>eval</code> even where <code>track.js</code> was blocked. That is what happened at HSBC. Its CSP blocked <code>track.js</code> and the data theft, but the inline copy still ran.</li></ul><p><strong><code>connect-src</code> matters.</strong> The 17 "partial" sites blocked card, cookie and screenshot theft because they restrict where the page can send data. That doesn't stop address swapping, which happens inside the page.</p><h2 id="what-to-do-now">What to do now</h2><p><strong>If your site uses Optimizely Web:</strong></p><ol><li>Open every project's <strong>Project JavaScript</strong> and every experiment's custom code. Look for <code>1688360.cc</code>, <code>atob(</code> and any script you didn't write.</li><li>Review who and what has write access to your Optimizely account (users, API tokens, integrations) and rotate credentials.</li><li>If you have card fields in your own forms, treat cards entered on 16 November as potentially exposed.</li><li>Treat any session tokens or secrets in non-<code>HttpOnly</code> cookies as exposed, and rotate them.</li><li>Deploy a CSP. Restrict <code>connect-src</code>, avoid <code>'unsafe-eval'</code> where you can, and turn on reporting.</li><li>Consider if you need to communicate to your customers about having served them malware.</li></ol><p><strong>If you visited an affected site on 16 November:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Update your iPhone or iPad</strong> to iOS 18.7.3 or later, or iOS 26.3 or later.</li><li><strong>Check any crypto transfer</strong> you made. If the address you pasted doesn't match the one you meant to pay, it may have been swapped.</li><li><strong>Watch your card statements</strong> if you paid by card on an affected site.</li></ol><h2 id="indicators-of-compromise">Indicators of compromise</h2><p>All of these domains are attacker-owned; block them including subdomains.</p>
