<p><strong>Attacker wallets: </strong><br>BTC <code>19g9rZcPeiz8rUvyWpTRyXXhPnjsWrc3kW</code><br>ETH/EVM <code>0xC4026b06D82b0F0B363eda0642D61CfbDc17884e</code><br>TRON <code>THvs6p56XkUiMkJeSc51cAP64u2RsTYqxa</code><br>SOL <code>FVtL8NMYS9PA1v3HE67XUGCtGA9JaZUvqav2fU2LrV5n</code><br>TON <code>UQAgM1VqSZQiJLSG5w-ovurq5CZdxLEK3_7CMmwALTA5e09p</code><br>LTC <code>ltc1q3wp7g6ds0ggg6fl6kncpa4sepn0q3qfacgred5</code><br>BCH <code>qzkaaf8n6grmpr0d9lx8xutn3r9lk722f566hhpe58</code><br>DOGE <code>DBEGrxRF7KMSuhTS4c1MFviStU6efdMnu5</code></p><p>We keep an archive of every infected snippet with hashes and timestamps, and can share it with affected organisations and CERTs on request. During research and investigation, once we were confident that Optimizely was the source, we began efforts to reach out via various channels and provide the full details. At the time of publication, we have not had any response. </p>
